 @enchantress7928  No idea why you ask me. :P
But I have an opinion. That is that I don't know what to think about the RTX2060, yet. It clearly isn't a mainstream product because it is way overpriced for that. Its performance per dollar/euro however is better than a GTX1070Ti. Not that this is a hard thing to accomplish seeing as the GTX1070Ti was overpriced as well. RT cores on the RTX2060 are useless. They can't real-time ray trace anything and we know that already. DLSS can be nice, but it isn't that amazing or a dealmaker. 

This all doesn't stack up that good for the RTX2060. But I can't really judge yet because I am rather old fashioned about video cards. I much rather compare them to the current generation competition rather than last gen. Navi however isn't out there. If Navi is even semi decent, it should probably match or beat the RTX2060. Knowing AMD a bit, it should also cost a lot less for that level of performance.

 @alexanderdaum8053  I saw the CES Keynote. ;)
Didn't expect Vega 20 (that is the chip of RX Vega VII, look it up) to show up in a gaming product. It probably is around the RTX2080 with 1440p and 4k. 1080p, i doubt it. Then again it should be faster under DX12 or Vulkan than a RX2080. Price will probably be similar. AMD doesn't have much options with 16GB of HBM2 either. I do suspect that RX Vega VII will sell better as a workstation card than a gaming card, but a sale is a sale. RX Vega VII should be very close to the absolutely huge GV100 from Nvidia. A cut down version of that card is the Titan V and RX Vega VII very well could be a bit faster and far cheaper.

Don't know what happened to Navi. I still think it will be H1/2019, but AMD clearly decided it was not ready for an announcement. Doesn't chance the fact that I am not exactly exited about Turing in general. We got two very big chips (TU-106 and TU-104) and the absolutely huge TU-102 for the RTX2080Ti which is not even the full chip, no that is a cut down chip Nvidia sells for that much. A TU-102 will be very expensive to make, but not that expensive. All these Turing chips are filled with a lot of largely useless hardware and are way overpriced for there level of performance. I can't exactly call that great.

