@Imasundaj while I see where you're coming from, there are nefarious short term incentives to spread misinformation in many information sharing industries. All journalists have access to sources and information that we the public don't have. Every article or story they produce could gain more clicks/views/publicity if they exaggerate the facts or implications.  Every day journalists are incentivised to bend the truth. Does that mean we should mistrust all journalists? 

I'm just starting out in academia. My colleagues and I could undoubtedly gain more attention from our research by manipulating our data. Every day academics are incentivised to bend the truth. Does that mean we should mistrust all academics? 

Obviously we should have a degree of scepticism because some media outlets, techtubers, journalists and academics aren't trustworthy. I also agree 100% that this situation gets much worse if negative reviews start getting punished by vendors. But ultimately we are responsible for choosing who we trust. @GN and @HU are my two go-to sources for honest perspectives on the industry.

 @supermadkid  Slightly different situation with KitGuru. Firstly, several other tech outlets also missed out on getting a sample in time for launch, as apparently, so the story goes, there were only a very small number of Fury-X's available for all of Europe's tech media ( it sounds dumb, but this is AMD we're talking about here, back when they were nearly broke). Press bias was a much bigger issue, and more pervasive then than today. They had just recently released a scathing "speculative" video about the upcoming 300 series, including Fury. Of course they weren't going to be top of the list of recipients if there weren't enough cards to go around (whatever issues may have existed between the two companies were subsequently resolved, because KitGuru got a sample of the Fury Nano in time to provide a launch day review not long after).  Nvidia, on the other hand, has no issues with supplying enough cards. They just don't want to.

Secondly, AMD proactively called them and told them explicitly what was happening well in advance.  They were able to post an article announcing there wouldn't be a launch day review a week before the card launched. In Steve's case he was completely dicked around. He was told he was getting a card and it was in the mail, then fobbed off with vague excuses, and kept in the dark about what was going on and when the real NDA was lifting for reviews. He had no idea that other people's reviews were coming so soon.

In other words, they lied to him, and deliberately tried to make a fool out of him. It's not quite the same thing.

